.TH "SKARN" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.33.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn \- AI coding session security scanner with built\-in session search
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBskarn\fR \fBcheck\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBassess\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBvet\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBmcp\fR
\fBskarn\fR \fBbaseline\fR \fIaudit|accept\fR \fIfile\fR [<fingerprint>] [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBsearch\fR \fIquery\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBrecent\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBrestore\fR \fIsession\fR
\fBskarn\fR \fBmessages\fR \fIsession\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBstats\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBtools\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBmcps\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBcmds\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBexport\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBguard\fR [report|accept <fingerprint>] [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBsetup\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBdoctor\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBcompletion\fR \fIshell\fR
\fBskarn\fR \fBserve\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBtaxonomies\fR
\fBskarn\fR \fBlicense\fR [<file>|\-|renew] [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBeula\fR [accept]
\fBskarn\fR \fBfleet\fR \fIaggregate|readout\fR \fIpath\fR [options]
skarn \fB\-\-version\fR
skarn \fB\-\-help\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Skarn is an AI coding session security scanner with built\-in session search.
It detects leaked credentials and the attack patterns that cause or exploit
them in the local session transcripts of AI coding assistants (Claude Code,
Gemini CLI, Antigravity, Codex CLI, Cursor, VS Code Copilot Chat, Kimi Code
CLI, Grok Build, Grok Bot, OpenCode, and Cline, plus Claude Code and Cowork
transcripts exported from a Claude Enterprise organization through the
Compliance API), and it lets you search, browse, and analyze those sessions.
.PP
Skarn is a single static binary. It scans locally and makes no network
connection by default; the only features that transmit data off the machine
are opt\-in and named explicitly in the SECURITY section below.
.PP
Skarn has two faces over one parsing engine. The recall commands (search,
recent, stats, and friends) are the daily use surface. They match and filter
on the raw session text, then mask every detected secret in what they print,
which runs the detector over the sessions a command emits: a value the
detector matches in any session of that output is masked in every session of
it, while a value it matches nowhere in that set prints as recorded. A session
that is still being written when a command reads it is stamped, scanned and
read again, up to three times, so what prints always comes from bytes the scan
covered; one that keeps changing is left out of that run with a count on
stderr, and a single\-session view of it asks you to re\-run once it is quiet.
The check command is the security scanner: it runs the detection rules,
correlates attack chains, and computes a session risk score for CI/CD gating.
Every finding is mapped to MITRE ATLAS, the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications
2025, and CWE; run skarn taxonomies to print the crosswalk and the pinned
framework versions.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBcheck\fR [options]
Scan AI sessions for leaked credentials and attack patterns.
.TP
\fBassess\fR [options]
Scan every AI session, print a redacted summary. Free, no license.
.TP
\fBvet\fR [options]
Vet the local AI config, read\-only and offline. Free, no license.
.TP
\fBmcp\fR
Local stdio MCP server: read\-only, redacted scan and config tools.
.TP
\fBbaseline\fR \fIaudit|accept\fR \fIfile\fR [<fingerprint>] [options]
Triage into a baseline (audit); accept a false positive (accept).
.TP
\fBsearch\fR \fIquery\fR [options]
Search past AI sessions (literal text, or \-\-regex).
.TP
\fBrecent\fR [options]
List recent sessions.
.TP
\fBrestore\fR \fIsession\fR
Restore a session.
.TP
\fBmessages\fR \fIsession\fR [options]
Show the messages of a session.
.TP
\fBstats\fR [options]
Session analytics: tokens, models, tools, and timing.
.TP
\fBtools\fR [options]
Show the tools used across sessions.
.TP
\fBmcps\fR [options]
Show the MCP calls made across sessions.
.TP
\fBcmds\fR [options]
Show the shell commands run across sessions.
.TP
\fBexport\fR [options]
Export sessions as text, json, ndjson, html; secrets masked.
.TP
\fBguard\fR [report|accept <fingerprint>] [options]
Pre\-execution hook: read an event on stdin, emit a verdict.
.TP
\fBsetup\fR [options]
Wire the guard hook into detected assistants, then self\-test it.
.TP
\fBdoctor\fR [options]
Check whether skarn is actually protecting this machine.
.TP
\fBcompletion\fR \fIshell\fR
Print a completion script for bash, zsh, fish, or powershell.
PowerShell is the fourth shell: `skarn completion powershell` prints a script
that registers a native argument completer for skarn and skarn.exe, to
dot\-source from a PowerShell profile (Windows PowerShell 5.1 and PowerShell
7). Every script is generated from the same command model as this manual, so
each one completes every verb and flag it documents. A shell name outside
bash, zsh, fish, and powershell prints the supported list on stderr and exits
1.
.TP
\fBserve\fR [options]
Local web UI on 127.0.0.1, with every detected secret masked.
.TP
\fBtaxonomies\fR
Print the crosswalk plus Cyber Resilience Act process evidence.
Print the standards crosswalk (MITRE ATLAS, OWASP LLM Top 10, CWE) and the EU
Cyber Resilience Act obligation axis (process evidence relevant to an
obligation, not proof of product conformity).
.TP
\fBlicense\fR [<file>|\-|renew] [options]
Show the active license, install one, or renew it.
.TP
\fBeula\fR [accept]
Print the End User License Agreement, or record acceptance.
.TP
\fBfleet\fR \fIaggregate|readout\fR \fIpath\fR [options]
Fold drops into the org aggregate; render the executive readout.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Check options"
See \fBskarn\-check\fR(1).
.TP
\fB\-\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a custom TOML rules file in addition to the bundled rules.
.TP
\fB\-\-ai\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load an AI\-specific rules file.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ai\-rules\fR
Disable the AI\-specific rules.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-default\-rules\fR
Disable the bundled detection rules.
.TP
\fB\-\-feed\fR \fIpath\fR
Apply a signed maintained\-feed bundle over the baseline rules. Requires a Pro
license.
.TP
\fB\-\-update\-rules\fR
Fetch and verify the latest feed from the subscriber channel, then scan.
Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-feed\-url\fR \fIurl\fR
Feed channel URL for \-\-update\-rules; falls back to $SKARN_FEED_URL. It must
be https, with plain http accepted only against loopback, and must carry no
userinfo. Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Disable all network access (the maintained\-feed fetch).
.TP
\fB\-\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Report only findings at or above this severity. One of: low, medium, high,
critical. The default is medium.
.TP
\fB\-\-canary\-file\fR \fIpath\fR
File of planted canary tokens, one per line; a match is a proven breach.
.TP
\fB\-\-check\-packages\fR
Flag typosquatted or URL\-sourced package installs found in sessions
(offline).
.TP
\fB\-\-check\-code\fR
Flag insecure code written by the assistant during the session.
.TP
\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIn\fR
Scan window in hours; 0 means no time limit. The default is 720.
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the scan to one assistant's sessions. If this machine has no tool by
that name, skarn exits 6 rather than scanning nothing. A custom tool declared
in ~/.skarn.json is accepted by its configured name. One of: claude, gemini,
codex, cursor, copilot, kimi, grok, grokbot, antigravity, opencode, cline.
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict the scan to matching projects: a comma\-separated list of names or
paths, matched case\-insensitively against a session's project display name,
its canonical root path, or a parent directory of that root. A session read
from a Compliance API export carries no project, so any \-\-project value
excludes it.
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fIid\fR
Scan a single session by id.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
Output format. ndjson is an ECS\-shaped event stream, one redacted finding per
line, for SIEM ingest. A finding read from a Compliance API export carries the
user id as user.id and the workspace as skarn.workspace_id, both as recorded,
and the email address as user.email fixed to **** when the session carries one
and omitted when it does not, so the mask says an address exists and is
withheld rather than that none was recorded. sarif (partialFingerprints),
ndjson (skarn.fingerprint), evidence, and the json cross\-session chains
(fingerprint and exfilFingerprint) carry the finding fingerprint: the sha256
of the rule id, a NUL byte, and the lowercase hex sha256 of the
ASCII\-lowercased secret. That hash is deterministic and unkeyed, so a
guessable secret is recoverable from it by candidate testing wherever the
output travels. evidence is a redacted Markdown evidence pack for a product
technical file, and the one value that requires a Pro license. One of: text,
json, sarif, ndjson, evidence. The default is text.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Verbose output.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable colored output.
.TP
\fB\-\-policy\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a declarative TOML policy file: fail thresholds, required/forbidden
rules, baseline enforcement, and the output profile. The \-\-fail\-on\-* and
\-\-profile flags override it. A file skarn cannot read or parse exits 4
rather than falling back to the embedded default, which gates nothing.
Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-profile\fR \fIpreset\fR
Output profile. enterprise keeps high\-confidence findings and dedups them.
One of: enterprise. Requires an Enterprise license.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Exit 1 if any finding is at or above this severity. One of: low, medium, high,
critical.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-risk\fR \fI0\-100\fR
Exit 2 if the session risk score exceeds this threshold.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error\fR
Fail closed with exit 6 when the scan was incomplete: a tool source failed
discovery, a session store that exists could not be read, a session failed to
parse, a session had truncated or compacted transcript content so it no longer
holds the whole conversation (the assistant compacted it before the scan, or a
compliance transcript export carried a truncated tool block, a truncated text
block, or a compaction marker), a compliance transcript export withheld a turn
as unavailable or carried a client\-asserted turn the feed did not verify, or
the scan read no sessions. Truncated or compacted content counts sessions,
because a cut block is a partial turn and nobody can count what a compaction
removed; withheld and unverified content counts turns, because the export
names each one. Every truncated tool block counts its session, including a
block returned as a placeholder instead of cut text, so a coverage gap is
over\-reported rather than missed. By default a partial scan still reports,
and an empty scan still warns on stderr.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\fR \fIpath\fR
Suppress findings recorded in this baseline, so the scan fails only on new
ones. A single file is free; a directory unions every .json baseline inside
it, an org\-shared accepted set, and requires a Pro license. That union leaves
out any fingerprint a member labelled a true positive. Without this flag skarn
applies ~/.config/skarn/baseline.json when it exists, for suppression only;
the \-\-baseline\-create and \-\-baseline\-merge targets stay explicit.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\-create\fR
Write the current findings to the \-\-baseline file as the accepted set, then
exit. Under \-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error an incomplete scan is refused instead:
the run exits 6 and leaves the existing file as it was.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\-merge\fR \fIfile\fR
Consolidate the \-\-baseline input (a file or a directory of per\-member
baselines) into one distributable file and exit. Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-audit\-log\fR \fIfile\fR
Append a hash\-chained record of this scan (timestamp, policy, finding counts,
verdict; no secrets) to this local log. The chain detects in\-place edits and
reordering of recorded history. Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-audit\-verify\fR \fIfile\fR
Verify an audit log's hash chain, then exit without scanning. A broken chain
exits 1. A log that exists but could not be read exits 6: a permission
failure, a directory, or a log past the read cap. An absent log is a valid
empty chain and exits 0. Needs no license.
.TP
\fB\-\-drop\fR \fIdir\fR
After the scan, write a self\-describing drop artifact to
<dir>/<identity>/<runId>.json: identity, org, host, run id, scan window, the
\-\-project scope it declares, and the redacted summary counts. The counts
cover the declared scope, not the machine, so the session count is the number
that passed \-\-project and \-\-session. Scope is recorded twice:
scope.projects is the \-\-project needles verbatim, often a path, and
scope.resolved is the project names those needles matched. Content is
attributed against scope.resolved, so a run that declared no scope resolves
nothing and therefore names no project at all. The assistant list and the
last\-activity timestamp describe the sessions the scan read inside that
scope, not the ones that produced a finding. The guard mode comes from the
same wired\-hook walk doctor performs, which only this flag pays for; an
absent guard mode means it could not be resolved, not that no hook is wired.
The write is atomic, so a second run in the same window adds a file rather
than overwriting one. The artifact carries a schema version and is never
reinterpreted across versions, so a release that moves that version needs the
machines upgraded alongside whatever assembles their artifacts, and the ones
written at the older version cleared out of the collection directory. Skarn
does not transmit it; moving the directory is the organization's job. Identity
comes from SKARN_IDENTITY, falling back to a hostname\-derived tag with a
warning.
.TP
\fB\-\-product\fR \fIname\fR
Release lineage for \-\-format evidence: the product this scan's sessions
changed. Without lineage, the evidence pack states that it cannot be attached
to a specific technical file.
.TP
\fB\-\-product\-version\fR \fIver\fR
Release lineage for \-\-format evidence: the product version the pack
describes.
.TP
\fB\-\-build\-id\fR \fIid\fR
Release lineage for \-\-format evidence: the commit or build identifier the
pack describes.
.TP
\fB\-\-sbom\-ref\fR \fIref\fR
Release lineage for \-\-format evidence: a reference to the product's declared
SBOM, recorded as supplied (control characters masked). Skarn neither parses
nor fetches it.
.TP
\fB\-\-sbom\fR \fIpath\fR
Reconcile production dependencies the sessions added to a manifest against
this declared SBOM, in CycloneDX or SPDX 2.x JSON format. Each undeclared
addition is reported as low\-severity workflow\-drift evidence, so pair it
with \-\-severity low. Unlike \-\-sbom\-ref, skarn reads and parses the file;
an unreadable or malformed one fails the scan.
.TP
\fB\-\-user\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the scan to matching users: a comma\-separated list of user ids or
email addresses, where an id matches exactly and an email address matches
case\-insensitively. Only a session read from a Compliance API export carries
either, so on a machine without an export, or under a registered license below
Enterprise, every \-\-user value matches nothing and the report is empty at
exit 0. The drop artifact and the evidence pack do not record a user
narrowing, so a narrowed run reads like a whole\-corpus one there.
.SS "Assess options"
See \fBskarn\-assess\fR(1).
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-out\fR \fIfile\fR
Write a self\-contained, redacted share report to this file; .md or .markdown
renders Markdown, any other extension renders HTML. The report names a session
file by its basename and a project by its display name, and writes a home
directory inside a code\-context snippet as ~.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit the redacted scan as JSON (the same shape as the serve /api/scan view)
for scripting, instead of the friendly summary. With \-\-dossier the body is
the dossier envelope that wraps this scan, not the bare scan shape.
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Disable all network access. Assess is already egress\-free; this is the
belt\-and\-suspenders guarantee.
.TP
\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIn\fR
Scan window in hours; 0 (the default) scans every session on the machine. The
default is 0.
.TP
\fB\-\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Only summarize findings at or above this severity; the default (low) scans
everything and curates the display. One of: low, medium, high, critical. The
default is low.
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the scan to one assistant's sessions. If this machine has no tool by
that name, skarn exits 6 rather than scanning nothing. A custom tool declared
in ~/.skarn.json is accepted by its configured name. One of: claude, gemini,
codex, cursor, copilot, kimi, grok, grokbot, antigravity, opencode, cline.
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict the scan to matching projects: a comma\-separated list of names or
paths, matched case\-insensitively against a session's project display name,
its canonical root path, or a parent directory of that root. A session read
from a Compliance API export carries no project, so any \-\-project value
excludes it.
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fIid\fR
Assess a single session by id.
.TP
\fB\-\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a custom TOML rules file in addition to the bundled rules.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\fR \fIpath\fR
Suppress findings recorded in this accepted\-findings baseline. Without this
flag, ~/.config/skarn/baseline.json is applied automatically when it exists. A
directory of baselines requires a Pro license; assess never gates, so an
unlicensed directory degrades to a warning and an unsuppressed scan.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable colored output.
.TP
\fB\-\-dossier\fR \fIselector\fR
Scope \-o and \-\-json to one incident dossier: one finding by its
64\-character fingerprint, or one session by id, auto\-detected by shape. A
reused credential matches every session it appears in. The dossier holds the
redacted facts Skarn has for the selection, plus an explicit list of the CRA
Article 14 reporting fields Skarn cannot supply. It is a local share artifact;
Skarn does not file reports or determine severity. Every format names a
project by its display name. The HTML and JSON dossiers name a session file by
its basename, write a home directory inside a code\-context snippet as ~, and
leave out the project root and the user and workspace ids; the Markdown
dossier prints none of those fields.
.TP
\fB\-\-user\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the scan to matching users: a comma\-separated list of user ids or
email addresses, where an id matches exactly and an email address matches
case\-insensitively. Only a session read from a Compliance API export carries
either, so on a machine without an export, or without the Enterprise license
that reading one needs, every \-\-user value matches nothing.
.SS "Vet options"
See \fBskarn\-vet\fR(1).
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
Output format. json and sarif carry the same rule ids, severities, config
paths, and locators as the text list. One of: text, json, sarif. The default
is text.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Exit 1 if any finding is at or above this severity. Without it, vet always
exits 0: it surfaces, it does not gate. One of: low, medium, high, critical.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error\fR
Fail closed with exit 6 when a config file that exists was not read: it could
not be read, or it was skipped because it is not that assistant's live
configuration. A partial view of the configuration is never reported as clean.
Implied by \-\-fail\-on\-severity. A home directory that cannot be resolved at
all exits 6 regardless of this flag, since nothing was examined.
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Disable all network access. Vet never makes a network call; this is the
belt\-and\-suspenders guarantee.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable colored output.
.SS "Mcp options"
See \fBskarn\-mcp\fR(1).
.TP
\fB\-\-enable\-recall\fR
Add the content tools search_sessions and get_session to the server. Off by
default. They return transcript content with detected credentials masked and
other text as recorded, masking a detected value in every session of the
response rather than only where it was detected, so anything they return
leaves the machine through the connected MCP client and reaches the model
provider. The search_sessions response carries no echo of the query or of the
project filter, and its skipped count names how many sessions were left out
because their file changed on disk while it was being read; those sessions
contribute no row rather than being masked from stale bytes.
.SS "Baseline options"
See \fBskarn\-baseline\fR(1).
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fItext\fR
Reason recorded with an accepted finding (audit prompts for one; accept takes
it as a flag).
.TP
\fB\-\-identity\fR \fIid\fR
Override the accepted\-by identity stamped on the decision; defaults to
$SKARN_IDENTITY.
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the audit scan to one assistant's sessions. One of: claude, gemini,
codex, cursor, copilot, kimi, grok, grokbot, antigravity, opencode, cline.
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict the audit scan to matching projects: a comma\-separated list of names
or paths, matched case\-insensitively against a session's project display
name, its canonical root path, or a parent directory of that root. A session
read from a Compliance API export carries no project, so any \-\-project value
excludes it.
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fIid\fR
Audit a single session by id.
.TP
\fB\-\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a custom TOML rules file for the audit scan in addition to the bundled
rules.
.TP
\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIn\fR
Audit scan window in hours; 0 means no time limit. The default is 720.
.TP
\fB\-\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Only audit findings at or above this severity. One of: low, medium, high,
critical. The default is medium.
.TP
\fB\-\-user\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the audit scan to matching users: a comma\-separated list of user ids
or email addresses, where an id matches exactly and an email address matches
case\-insensitively. Only a session read from a Compliance API export carries
either, so on a machine without an export, or without the Enterprise license
that reading one needs, every \-\-user value matches nothing.
.SS "Guard options"
See \fBskarn\-guard\fR(1).
.TP
\fB\-\-guard\-mode\fR \fImode\fR
audit reports only; enforce emits a deny or ask verdict. Enforce needs a
paid\-tier license; an unlicensed guard, or one on the free tier, runs
audit\-only. One of: audit, enforce. The default is audit.
.TP
\fB\-\-strict\fR
Also gate Read operations (credential\-file reconnaissance).
.TP
\fB\-\-agent\fR \fIname\fR
Hook host. By default, auto\-detect from the event shape. Pass codex for Codex
CLI and copilot for Copilot CLI: both send Claude\-shaped events that skarn
cannot tell apart from Claude's. Gemini CLI, Grok Build, the Antigravity CLI,
Kimi Code and Cline each send a payload key no other host sends, so they
auto\-detect too, and the shipped configs pin them anyway. On Kimi Code that
pin is load\-bearing rather than uniform policy: a host embedding kimi's older
hook engine sends no client_type, and its Claude\-shaped prompt event then
fails open under the Claude adapter. On Cline the shipped executables pin
\-\-agent cline all the same, because a hook file copied between machines and
forks carries nothing else that names the host. Grok Build is the one host
that overrides an explicit pin: an event delivered by grok's own hook runner
is scanned by the grok adapter even where the invoking config pins another
host, because grok runs Claude\-config and Cursor\-config hooks with its own
payload dialect. With \-\-self\-test, all (the default) tests the claude,
cursor, codex, copilot, gemini, grok, antigravity, kimi, and cline adapters,
copilot on both its CLI and VS Code dialects. With report, this option
restricts the summary to one assistant, repeatable or comma\-separated. One
of: claude, cursor, codex, copilot, gemini, grok, antigravity, kimi, cline,
all.
.TP
\fB\-\-event\fR \fIname\fR
The hook event this config entry fires on, for a host whose payload does not
name its own event. The Copilot CLI needs it: its permissionRequest payload
carries a subset of preToolUse's documented fields and nothing separates the
two, so a permissionRequest entry must say so or the guard reads it as a
preToolUse and answers in a schema that event does not understand. Default:
the event is inferred from the payload shape, which never yields
permissionRequest. Codex names its own event and the pin never overrides that
name; skarn reads the pin only where the name is missing, so a strict deny for
a payload that never parsed, or a call carrying no hook_event_name, takes the
pinned shape instead of the PreToolUse default. Ignored by every other host,
and by the VS Code Copilot dialect. One of: preToolUse, permissionRequest.
.TP
\fB\-\-self\-test\fR
Verify the guard's own code path instead of reading an event. Skarn feeds two
synthetic fixture events through each adapter's real code path, one benign and
one carrying a fake credential, then reports the verdicts, latency, and a
guard\-log write probe. The kimi pair carries kimi's own client_type stamp and
content\-block prompt shape, so the fixture proves the routing the shipped pin
also asserts. The mode in the header is this invocation's, from
\-\-guard\-mode and the license; it is never read from a host config, so a
bare run reports audit whatever skarn setup wired, and skarn doctor is where
the wired mode is reported. The fake credential is only ever printed redacted,
and the self\-test deliberately ignores the accepted\-findings baseline, so an
accepted fingerprint can never make a dead hook look alive. What it proves is
the binary, the adapter and the log write. It runs entirely in this process
and reads no host config, so it never proves that a host is calling the hook:
skarn doctor reads the host configs for that, and on a host doctor does not
cover (kimi today) the only proof is a flagged action driven through the host
itself. Exit 0 when every adapter passes, 1 otherwise; works unlicensed, with
audit semantics.
.TP
\fB\-\-log\fR \fIpath\fR
On the hook invocation, `skarn guard \-\-log <path|none>` names where this
call appends its redacted JSONL record. A path takes precedence over
$SKARN_GUARD_LOG; none writes no record even when that variable is set; no
flag at all falls back to the variable. Skarn refuses a path that expands to a
parent\-directory segment or carries a control character: no record is written
and there is no fallback destination, because a fallback would derive from
$XDG_CACHE_HOME or $HOME, which the same hook command can also set. With
report: a guard log to summarize, repeatable. Default: $SKARN_GUARD_LOG when
set, otherwise the log skarn setup wires for each assistant
($XDG_CACHE_HOME/skarn/guard\-<agent>\-audit.jsonl, plus the home cache beside
it when a log already exists there). A log that does not exist is named in the
output with the command that wires it, never silently counted as zero, and a
configured path the guard refuses is named as refused rather than shown as
empty.
.TP
\fB\-\-window\fR \fIspan\fR
With report: how far back to summarize, as <N>d or all. The default is 7d.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
With report: text renders the summary, the enforce\-cost forecast, and the
recommendation. json emits the whole aggregate as one object with stable field
names for fleet scripts, including a forecast object and the denominator
string that names what its rates are rates of. One of: text, json. The default
is text.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\fR \fIfile\fR
With accept: the accepted\-findings file to write. Defaults to the personal
baseline the guard itself reads (~/.config/skarn/baseline.json, honoring
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME), created on first use. `skarn baseline accept` always names
its file instead, because an accept the guard does not read would not stop the
block. Always a file, never a directory: the org\-union form is not reachable
from the guard. Accepting a fingerprint already recorded as a confirmed true
positive is refused, so a fingerprint\-only accept can never re\-silence a
real leak.
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fItext\fR
With accept: why this finding is a false positive, recorded in the baseline
entry. Do not paste a secret here; the text is stored verbatim.
.TP
\fB\-\-identity\fR \fIid\fR
With accept: override the accepted\-by identity stamped on the entry; defaults
to $SKARN_IDENTITY.
.SS "Setup options"
See \fBskarn\-setup\fR(1).
.TP
\fB\-\-agent\fR \fIname\fR
Target assistants, repeatable or comma\-separated. Default: all detected (an
assistant is detected when its config directory exists or its CLI is on PATH);
naming an assistant explicitly also targets it when undetected. One of:
claude, cursor, codex, copilot, gemini, grok, all.
.TP
\fB\-\-mode\fR \fImode\fR
Guard mode written into the hook commands. Audit records would\-be verdicts to
the guard log without blocking; start there, review the log, then flip to
enforce. Enforce needs a paid\-tier license to actually block; an unlicensed
guard, or one on the free tier, runs audit\-only regardless. One of: audit,
enforce. The default is audit.
.TP
\fB\-\-strict\fR
Add \-\-strict to the generated guard commands (also gates Read operations;
fail\-closed semantics on Codex).
.TP
\fB\-\-log\fR \fIpath\fR
Guard log path wired into the hook commands: a SKARN_GUARD_LOG= prefix on the
POSIX arms and on Copilot's powershell arm, and a \-\-log flag on the Codex
commandWindows arm, which Codex may run under either PowerShell or cmd.exe and
where no shell\-syntax prefix parses under both. Pass none to disable logging.
At \-\-scope project skarn refuses an explicit path, because a committed
config runs on every teammate's machine and carries only the portable
per\-user default. A path with a parent\-directory segment or a control
character is refused at any scope, since the guard refuses it at runtime and
the hook would write nothing. An explicit path reaches only the arms whose
host could have that location: a POSIX\-absolute path reaches the POSIX arms,
a drive\-qualified or UNC path reaches the Windows arm, and the other arm
keeps the portable default rather than a destination its host cannot resolve.
A path that does reach the Codex Windows arm may use only letters, digits and
~ / \e . \- _ : ; anything else is refused rather than escaped, because a bare
argument both shells must parse has no single correct escaping. The default is
~/.cache/skarn/guard\-<agent>\-audit.jsonl.
.TP
\fB\-\-command\fR \fIpath\fR
The guard executable invoked by the hook commands. At user scope the default
resolves this binary's absolute path, preferring the stable Homebrew
<prefix>/bin symlink over the versioned Cellar path, because GUI\-launched
hosts like Cursor do not inherit brew's PATH; pass skarn to opt into
PATH\-relative. At project scope the default is the bare name skarn, and a
value containing a path separator is refused: a committed config must resolve
skarn from PATH on every machine. The default is this binary's absolute path.
.TP
\fB\-\-scope\fR \fIscope\fR
Write user\-level configs (~/.claude/settings.json, ~/.cursor/hooks.json,
~/.codex/hooks.json, ~/.copilot/hooks/skarn.json, ~/.gemini/settings.json,
~/.grok/hooks/skarn.json) or project\-level ones (./.claude, ./.cursor,
\&./.codex, ./.github/hooks/skarn.json, ./.gemini, ./.grok/hooks/skarn.json
relative to the working directory), meant to be committed and shared. The two
scopes write different command shapes on purpose: user scope bakes this
binary's absolute path, immune to a GUI host's missing PATH, and project scope
emits a portable PATH\-guarded command (if command \-v skarn ...; then ...;
else exit 0; fi) with a literal $HOME log path, so the committed file runs
unmodified on every teammate's machine and exits 0 where skarn is not
installed. Gemini and Grok are the two exceptions to that promise, because
each reads a single command field on every platform: the committed POSIX form
does not run under the PowerShell a native\-Windows teammate's host uses, the
hook exits non\-zero, the host treats that as a warning and continues, and
that machine is unprotected until it wires its own. The same caveat therefore
applies to a committed .grok/hooks/skarn.json, whose project hooks
additionally run only once the folder is trusted with /hooks\-trust. Copilot
honors $COPILOT_HOME as its user\-scope root, and grok honors $GROK_HOME. One
of: user, project. The default is user.
.TP
\fB\-\-plugin\fR
With \-\-scope project, claude only: write the committed plugin requirement
(the skarn entry in extraKnownMarketplaces plus
enabledPlugins["skarn\-guard@skarn"]) instead of hook entries, so every
contributor who opens the repo installs the skarn\-guard plugin from the
public marketplace. Mutually exclusive with the hooks route in both
directions: \-\-plugin removes any skarn\-owned hook entries it previously
wrote, and a later hooks\-route run removes the plugin keys, so the guard can
never fire twice per event. Refused at user scope, for non\-claude assistants,
and combined with \-\-update, \-\-mode, \-\-strict, \-\-log, or \-\-command,
because the plugin ships its own hook files. Foreign marketplaces and plugins
are never touched.
.TP
\fB\-\-print\fR
Print the final per\-assistant config to stdout and write nothing; the
paste\-it\-yourself and fleet\-template path.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
With \-\-print: json emits one {agent, path, content} envelope per assistant
for machine consumption. One of: text, json. The default is text.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Non\-interactive: accept every prompt's default. Required when running without
a terminal.
.TP
\fB\-\-update\fR
Only touch assistants whose config already carries skarn entries; the
audit\-to\-enforce flip is skarn setup \-\-update \-\-mode enforce. An owned
entry is replaced whole: any field you added to it is dropped, and a field a
newer template stopped writing stays gone. The one exception is a hook\-call
timeout you tuned yourself (timeout, or timeoutSec on copilot), carried across
only when its entry pairs unambiguously with the replacement and the value is
a positive integer within the host's range, and otherwise reset to the
template default. A hook you added inside skarn's own matcher group is kept
where you put it: skarn replaces its own entry beside yours instead of adding
a second group to the event.
.TP
\fB\-\-remove\fR
Surgically remove skarn\-owned hook entries, dropping any matcher groups and
event arrays they leave empty. Foreign hooks are untouched, the file itself is
never deleted, and backups from earlier runs are kept.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print a machine\-readable result summary (per assistant: path, action, backup,
self\-test result) instead of the text report.
.SS "Doctor options"
See \fBskarn\-doctor\fR(1).
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Add detail to every check: the paths searched, each wired hook command, the
per\-tool session counts, and the resolved Homebrew path.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit the full structured result: {version, checks[{id, status, detail, fix,
notes}], summary{pass, warn, fail}}, plus managed_root at the top level when
SKARN_MANAGED_ROOT is active, managed_registry_root when
SKARN_MANAGED_REGISTRY_ROOT is active, and an additive layers array ([{layer,
path, effect, lockdown, wired}]) on each agent_config_<agent> check, where a
Windows registry layer carries its policy key as the path. A rendered report
always exits 0; the two refusals that precede it are an unknown option (exit
1) and a home directory that cannot be resolved (exit 6).
.SS "Serve options"
See \fBskarn\-serve\fR(1).
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fIn\fR
Port to listen on; 0 asks the OS to assign one. Always binds 127.0.0.1 only.
The default is 7777.
.TP
\fB\-\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a custom TOML rules file in addition to the bundled rules; the web
Security scan then also surfaces its findings, and every value the pack
detects is masked in the recall views too.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\fR \fIpath\fR
Suppress findings recorded in this accepted\-findings baseline; the Security
view hides them by default behind a show/hide toggle. Without this flag,
~/.config/skarn/baseline.json is applied automatically when it exists. A
directory of baselines (the org form) requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-fleet\fR \fIpath\fR
Serve a Fleet roster view (preview) over this fleet.json aggregate of
per\-machine redacted scan artifacts. An external step produces the aggregate
from each machine's dropped scan output; skarn neither collects nor transmits
fleet data. When that step ran against an org\-declared machine roster, the
aggregate also carries the coverage that roster implies, and the view states
how many of the expected machines reported and names the ones that did not.
Assembled without a roster it carries none, because a machine that never
reported is invisible to anything reading only the artifacts that arrived.
Rendering an aggregate needs the Enterprise console entitlement: without it,
serve exits 5 before it binds a port and before it reads the file. With it,
serve validates the file against the current aggregate contract before it
binds a port. A file that declares a schemaVersion exits 1 when a machine's
topExposure names a project absent from the projects that machine resolved,
when a machine carries projects while declaring no scope, or when a
topExposure secret is not a redaction\-chokepoint preview. Every refusal names
the offending machine, and the two scope refusals name the project as well. A
file that declares no schemaVersion at all is refused the same way, naming the
aggregator version to regenerate it with: no invariant can be evaluated on a
file that does not say what it is. The kit that produces the aggregate \- the
aggregator, the per\-platform scheduling units, the managed guard configs, and
the rollout runbooks \- is published at integrations/fleet/ in
https://github.com/skarn\-security/skarn\-dist, where it downloads with no
credential and no license; only rendering the assembled aggregate here needs
one. Requires an Enterprise license.
.SS "Global options"
Available on a bare invocation.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version and exit.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help and exit.
.SS "Recent options"
See \fBskarn\-recent\fR(1).
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict to one assistant's sessions. One of: claude, gemini, codex, cursor,
copilot, kimi, grok, grokbot, antigravity, opencode, cline.
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict to matching projects: a comma\-separated list of names or paths,
matched case\-insensitively against a session's project display name, its
canonical root path, or a parent directory of that root. A session read from a
Compliance API export carries no project, so any \-\-project value excludes
it.
.TP
\fB\-\-worktree\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict to sessions whose working directory is under this path.
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fIdate\fR
Lower bound of the time window, applied to a session file's last\-modified
time rather than to when the session started, so a session begun before the
bound but written to inside it is included and a resumed session stays visible
under the day you worked on it. Takes priority over \-\-hours. Naming one
session by id changes this: `skarn search \-\-session <id>`, `skarn stats
<id>` and the other per\-session views ignore the window altogether, and
`skarn messages <id>` is the single command that applies it there, reading
BOTH bounds against the session's own start time rather than its file and
refusing an out\-of\-window session with exit 1 instead of filtering it out. A
bare YYYY\-MM\-DD means that day's midnight, shifted by \-\-tz\-offset; a full
ISO timestamp is used as given.
.TP
\fB\-\-until\fR \fIdate\fR
Upper bound of the time window, applied to when a session STARTED rather than
to when its file was last written, so a session that began after the bound is
excluded even if it was already running before it. The two bounds therefore
read different timestamps. Passing it without \-\-since also drops the
\-\-hours lower bound, so the window becomes all history up to the bound. A
bare YYYY\-MM\-DD covers that whole day through 23:59:59, shifted by
\-\-tz\-offset; a full ISO timestamp is used as given. A session whose start
time cannot be parsed is kept. A view that names one session by id ignores
this bound, except `skarn messages <id>`, which refuses a session past it with
exit 1 rather than dropping it from a list.
.TP
\fB\-\-tz\-offset\fR \fIminutes\fR
Timezone offset in minutes for date grouping and display. The default is 0.
.TP
\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIn\fR
Time window in hours; 0 means no limit. The default is 24.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
Output format. One of: text, json, csv. The default is text.
.TP
\fB\-\-include\-empty\fR
Include sessions with no user or assistant messages (off by default). A run
that hid any prints their count on stderr, and \-\-format json carries it as
empty_hidden beside session_count; a discovered session whose file could not
be parsed is counted the same way, on stderr and as unreadable, so a listing
never drops a discovered session silently; the Antigravity IDE side\-channel
bundle, which carries scanned values rather than a conversation, is one such
session.
.TP
\fB\-\-user\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict to matching users: a comma\-separated list of user ids or email
addresses, where an id matches exactly and an email address matches
case\-insensitively. Only a session read from a Compliance API export carries
either, so on a machine without an export, or without the Enterprise license
that reading one needs, every \-\-user value matches nothing.
.SS "Messages options"
See \fBskarn\-messages\fR(1).
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict to one assistant's sessions. One of: claude, gemini, codex, cursor,
