.SS "License options"
See \fBskarn\-license\fR(1).
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIpath\fR
Write the renewed license to this path instead of the config directory. Only
applies to `skarn license renew`; the file is written with owner\-only
permissions, and an existing file that is not a readable Skarn license is
never overwritten.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Replace an already\-installed license without asking. Required when installing
over an existing license from a script or from stdin, where there is no
terminal to confirm on.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print the license status as a single JSON object (state, tier, licensee, org,
seats, license id, issue and expiry dates, days remaining, and the source it
was resolved from). The license token is never included.
.SS "Fleet options"
See \fBskarn\-fleet\fR(1).
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIfile\fR
With aggregate: the aggregate file to write, required; it is written
atomically through a temporary file in the same directory, so a refused run
leaves no partial aggregate behind. With readout: write the rendered readout
there instead of standard output.
.TP
\fB\-\-roster\fR \fIfile\fR
With aggregate: the org\-controlled roster of the machines expected to report
and the projects each may report on. A drop from a machine the roster does not
name is refused; a drop that resolved a project the roster does not permit
that machine is refused naming both; an unscoped drop is accepted only from a
machine the roster marks allowUnscoped; and the aggregate gains a coverage
block naming every rostered machine that sent nothing. Without it the machine
set is discovered from the drops and a machine that never reported is
invisible.
.TP
\fB\-\-prior\fR \fIaggregate\fR
With readout: the previous period's aggregate. The readout then reports
movements against it; omitted, it states that this is the baseline
measurement. The prior must be for the same organization and older than the
current aggregate.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
With readout: emit the underlying comparison object (coverage, fleet totals,
per\-machine movement, the ranked risk list) instead of Markdown; without
\-\-prior the movement fields are null.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fR
Clean, or informational output only. For mcp: the client closed stdin, which
is the normal shutdown.
.TP
\fB1\fR
A finding at or above the \-\-fail\-on\-severity threshold was reported. For
setup and guard \-\-self\-test: a target was refused or a verification failed.
For doctor: at least one check FAILED (a warning never exits nonzero), or an
unknown option was passed, which is refused before any check runs. For
restore: the recorded working directory or session id could not be rendered
safely, or the working directory carries a detected secret, so the resume
command cannot be printed, nothing was printed to stdout and the refusal is
named on stderr. Also: the first\-run EULA prompt was declined, so the invoked
operation was not performed. For mcp: an unknown argument was passed, which is
refused before the protocol loop starts. For license: an install or a renewal
was refused or did not complete, for example because an argument was invalid,
a license did not verify, a replacement was declined, there was no license to
renew, the renewal endpoint was refused or could not be reached, or a file
could not be read or written. A failure before the new license is written
leaves the installed license as it was.
.TP
\fB2\fR
The session risk score exceeded the \-\-fail\-on\-risk threshold. For setup:
no terminal to prompt on and no \-\-yes; the exact non\-interactive command is
printed. For license: the license service declined the renewal that license
renew asked for, and nothing was written.
.TP
\fB3\fR
A canary token was triggered (a proven breach). This overrides every other
gate. For license: license renew refused the license the service returned,
because it did not verify, could not be used (revoked or expired, for
example), or did not supersede the license being renewed or the one already at
the destination, so nothing was written.
.TP
\fB4\fR
A policy precondition was not met: a required rule is missing, the policy
requires a baseline and none was supplied or the one supplied did not load, or
the \-\-policy file itself could not be read or parsed. A policy that never
loaded is never quietly replaced by the embedded default, which gates nothing.
.TP
\fB5\fR
A requested paid feature is not covered by the active license (a Pro or
Enterprise flag without a license that covers it). Refused before any scan.
With no usable license at all, check exits 7 before this gate is consulted.
.TP
\fB6\fR
The scan could not complete: session discovery or the scan itself failed,
including a home directory that could not be resolved so no session store was
ever located, or the detection rules could not be loaded so no scan was ever
defined. Under \-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error an otherwise\-complete run exits 6 as
well whenever coverage was partial: a tool source dropped out of discovery, a
session store that exists could not be read, a session's parse failed, the
assistant compacted a session before the scan so its transcript no longer
holds the whole conversation, or the run read no sessions at all. That verdict
is computed BEFORE the report is rendered, so the SARIF invocation exit code,
the evidence pack and the audit record all carry 6 rather than describing the
run as clean and then exiting nonzero. A \-\-baseline\-create run is refused
on the same condition before it writes anything, so an incomplete scan is
never recorded as the accepted set and the previous baseline survives. For
check \-\-audit\-verify: the audit log exists but could not be read, so the
hash chain was never examined (an absent log is a valid empty chain and exits
0). For vet: the home directory could not be resolved (so the configuration
was never located at all), or a config file that exists was not read \- it
could not be read, or it was skipped because it is not that assistant's live
configuration \- so the configuration was only partly seen. For doctor: the
home directory could not be resolved, so every per\-assistant check would have
read an empty machine \- a diagnostic that could not be produced is never
reported as a healthy one. Fail\-closed \- the result is not trustworthy, so
it is never reported as clean.
.TP
\fB7\fR
No usable license is present \- none was found via SKARN_LICENSE,
SKARN_LICENSE_FILE, or the installed file \- so check, baseline audit, fleet
aggregate or fleet readout refused before scanning or reading a file. The free
license is issued at https://getskarn.com/free after a quick email
confirmation and is verified offline. Also used when a license IS present but
cannot be used at all (revoked, tampered, or signed by an untrusted key).
assess, baseline accept, guard accept, the recall commands, guard, setup,
doctor, vet, mcp, license, taxonomies and check \-\-audit\-verify never exit
7.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBSKARN_SCAN_THREADS\fR
Cap the worker\-pool size for every parallel sweep (scan and recall). 1 forces
a fully serial run. Default is one worker per core, capped at 32. Output is
identical for any value; only latency changes.
.TP
\fBSKARN_CLAUDE_DIRS\fR
Colon\-separated list of additional Claude Code config directories to scan
beyond the default ~/.claude. Each entry is the dir that contains projects/.
Roots are de\-duplicated.
.TP
\fBSKARN_TZ_OFFSET\fR
Default timezone offset in hours applied when grouping recall and stats output
by time.
.TP
\fBSKARN_NO_TIMING\fR
Suppress the dim "Scanned N sessions" timing line that recall commands print
after a text run on a terminal. Already auto\-suppressed for json/sarif/csv
output and for non\-TTY stdout.
.TP
\fBSKARN_LICENSE_RENEW_URL\fR
License\-service endpoint `skarn license renew` posts to. Defaults to
https://api.getskarn.com/v1/license/renew; set it to route renewals through an
enterprise proxy. It must be https, with plain http accepted only against
loopback, and must carry no userinfo, so a poisoned environment can neither
redirect the license token to a cleartext endpoint nor hand credentials to the
one it names. No other command reads it.
.TP
\fBSKARN_GUARD_LOG\fR
Path to append a redacted JSONL verdict log for guard (opt\-in; off when
unset). One record per flagged call, carrying the fingerprint that `skarn
guard accept` takes; clean calls, and calls whose every finding was already
accepted, are not logged.
.TP
\fBGEMINI_CLI_SYSTEM_SETTINGS_PATH\fR
Gemini CLI's own override for the path of its managed system settings file.
When set it REPLACES the default path, and Gemini also takes its
system\-defaults file from that path's directory. Doctor resolves both gemini
managed layers through it, so the report names the files Gemini actually reads
rather than default files it is not.
.TP
\fBGEMINI_CLI_SYSTEM_DEFAULTS_PATH\fR
Gemini CLI's own override for the path of its system\-defaults file, the
lowest of its four settings layers. When set it REPLACES the path Gemini would
otherwise derive from the system settings file's directory, and doctor reads
the override.
.TP
\fBSKARN_IDENTITY\fR
Local\-first identity tag (an SSO subject, email, or CI principal) stamped as
provenance on baseline entries created with \-\-baseline\-create. Optional;
Skarn never authenticates it.
.TP
\fBSKARN_ORG\fR
Local\-first org or team tag stamped alongside SKARN_IDENTITY on baseline
provenance. Optional.
.TP
\fBSKARN_FEED_URL\fR
Default feed channel URL used by check \-\-update\-rules when \-\-feed\-url is
not given (Pro). It must be https, with plain http accepted only against
loopback, and must carry no userinfo, so a poisoned environment cannot
redirect the subscriber credential to a cleartext endpoint.
.TP
\fBSKARN_FEED_TOKEN\fR
Credential presented to the subscriber feed channel when fetching a maintained
feed (Pro).
.TP
\fBSKARN_EULA_ACCEPTED\fR
Set to 1 to suppress the first\-run EULA prompt and notice for this run
without recording anything (ephemeral environments: containers, CI). Running
Skarn constitutes acceptance either way; `skarn eula accept` records it
durably instead.
.TP
\fBSKARN_LICENSE\fR
License token, resolved before SKARN_LICENSE_FILE and the config path.
.TP
\fBSKARN_LICENSE_FILE\fR
Path to a file containing a license token.
.TP
\fBCLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR\fR
A Claude Code config directory visible in Skarn's environment; folded into the
claude source like an SKARN_CLAUDE_DIRS entry.
.TP
\fBCOPILOT_HOME\fR
GitHub Copilot CLI config directory visible in Skarn's environment. The CLI
itself treats it as REPLACING ~/.copilot, and vet follows that: it reads
mcp\-config.json, config.json and settings.json from the directory this names
and not from the default. Scanning deliberately diverges: the copilot source
scans this root AND the default ~/.copilot, because a scanner's subject is
every local store, and sessions stranded in an abandoned default root still
hold the secrets they held the day they were written. A store present under
both roots is two real files, and both are read: either may hold a secret the
other lost. Microsoft Scout sets this variable to ~/.scout/copilot in the
environment of the Copilot CLI child process it spawns, not in the user's
shell, so Skarn's own process never sees it; that root is declared in the
scanner rather than read from here, exactly as $CODEX_HOME and $KIMI_CODE_HOME
are declared rather than guessed.
.TP
\fBGROK_HOME\fR
Grok Build config directory visible in Skarn's environment; replaces the
default ~/.grok as grok's home. Setup writes its hook file under the directory
this names, doctor resolves grok's config, requirements, hooks, and
folder\-trust files from it, and the guard's own behavior does not depend on
it.
.TP
\fBGROK_FOLDER_TRUST\fR
Grok Build's folder\-trust switch. Set to 0 it ungates every project\-scope
hook, MCP server, and LSP server, the same as [folder_trust] enabled = false
in grok's config; doctor honors it, so a machine that turned the gate off is
not reported as having a silenced project hook layer.
.TP
\fBKIMI_CODE_HOME\fR
A Kimi Code CLI data directory visible in Skarn's environment; replaces the
default ~/.kimi\-code as the kimi root, folded into the kimi source,
de\-duplicated, resolved via expandPath. Each root contributes its sessions/
and user\-history/ children.
.TP
\fBGROK_HOME\fR
A Grok Build data directory visible in Skarn's environment; grok itself
REPLACES ~/.grok with $GROK_HOME rather than layering. Skarn folds both roots,
de\-duplicated, resolved via expandPath, and accepts each only when it has a
sessions/ child.
.TP
\fBCODEX_HOME\fR
A Codex CLI data directory visible in Skarn's environment; folded into the
codex source alongside the default ~/.codex, de\-duplicated, resolved via
expandPath. Each root contributes its sessions/ and archived_sessions/
children.
.TP
\fBSKARN_MANAGED_ROOT\fR
Prefix prepended to every managed (MDM/fleet) config path skarn reads, so a
fleet admin can validate a staged payload directory before pushing it to a
single machine (SKARN_MANAGED_ROOT=/tmp/stage skarn doctor). Empty or unset
means the live system paths. Doctor applies it to every managed hook config it
inspects, and vet applies it to the system Grok Build layers it reads at
/etc/grok. Whenever it is active, doctor echoes the root in the report header
and emits it as managed_root in \-\-json, so a staged report can never pass as
a report about the live machine. It is a filesystem prefix and cannot re\-root
a registry key; SKARN_MANAGED_REGISTRY_ROOT does that.
.TP
\fBSKARN_MANAGED_REGISTRY_ROOT\fR
Registry key doctor reads the Windows managed policy keys under, so an
administrator can validate a staged payload without touching live Group
Policy. For example, SKARN_MANAGED_REGISTRY_ROOT='HKCU\eSoftware\eSkarnStage'
makes doctor read
HKCU\eSoftware\eSkarnStage\eHKLM\eSOFTWARE\ePolicies\eClaudeCode. The hive
stays a key component under the staging root, so the HKLM and HKCU policies
stage to distinct keys instead of collapsing into one. Empty or unset means
the live policy keys. Whenever it is active, doctor echoes it in the report
header and emits it as managed_registry_root in \-\-json, so a staged report
can never pass as a report about the live machine. Only doctor reads it.
.TP
\fBGROK_HOME\fR
Grok Build's data and configuration directory, default ~/.grok. It relocates
the whole Grok home surface that vet reads \- config.toml,
managed_config.toml, requirements.toml, the hooks/ directory, and the
hooks\-paths registry \- so a relocated Grok home is examined rather than
reported clean over an unexamined one.
.TP
\fBXDG_CACHE_HOME\fR
Base directory for the rule and feed cache. Defaults to ~/.cache, so the cache
lives in ~/.cache/skarn/.
.TP
\fBSKARN_COMPLIANCE_EXPORT_DIR\fR
Export root scanned by the Compliance API session source. Reading the export
needs an Enterprise license: a run that reaches discovery under a lesser
entitlement (check and baseline audit under a registered Free, Pro or Team
license, assess and the recall verbs under any) counts the export sessions it
found, reads none of them, and every session\-reading surface prints the
notice `N compliance export session(s) found; reading them needs an Enterprise
license`, once per run on stderr and as a complianceWithheld or
compliance_withheld field where the surface is machine\-readable; the count is
never a partial\-scan verdict and never changes an exit code, and every other
session store on the machine scans as before. Set an absolute, non\-empty
path; a relative or empty value is refused and the source is dropped with a
warning, never resolved against the working directory. This root REPLACES the
default: exactly one root is scanned. Unlike COPILOT_HOME and GROK_HOME, it is
not folded in beside the default because this directory is Skarn's own
artifact, written by the exporter in integrations/anthropic\-compliance/.
There is no abandoned foreign default to keep visible, and selecting one
organization's export must not also scan a stale one. When unset, the root is
$XDG_DATA_HOME/skarn/compliance\-export if XDG_DATA_HOME is absolute, else
~/.local/share/skarn/compliance\-export/. A ~/.skarn.json tools.claude entry
with sessions_path repoints every builtin named claude, this store included;
sessions_path set to null disables them, and a based_on: claude clone yields
one clone per builtin named claude. The layout is
<root>/<organization_uuid>/<session_id>.json. The walk enters only directories
at the root and descends exactly one level; .watermark.json and
export\-log.jsonl at the root are never read. Inside an organization
directory, only regular *.json files with a non\-empty stem drawn from
[A\-Za\-z0\-9_] are read, and session.id must match that stem. The stem
charset is the exporter's contract and has not been validated against live
ids. A rejected filename, a symlinked session file or a session file with more
than one hard link raises the unreadable\-store count once per store as a
partial\-scan signal. Sessions carry no project and no cost: any \-\-project
value excludes them, stats records zero cost, and recent prints a dash. The
surface ids cowork and claude_enterprise are counted in stats but are not
\-\-cli values; use \-\-cli claude to select the store, while \-\-cli cowork
exits 6. An admin who exports their own organization and also runs Claude Code
locally sees their own sessions twice, once under a clls_ id and once under an
on\-disk UUID. Run the exporter on a machine without a local Claude store, or
scope with \-\-project. On Windows, the unset default is
%LOCALAPPDATA%\eskarn\ecompliance\-export, and XDG_DATA_HOME is not consulted.
LOCALAPPDATA must be an absolute path with a drive letter or a UNC prefix;
otherwise, the source is dropped and counted as one dropped source, exactly as
a relative SKARN_COMPLIANCE_EXPORT_DIR is: check and assess report the drop as
a partial scan, doctor reports a failed source, and the recall commands list
nothing for it. On Windows, SKARN_COMPLIANCE_EXPORT_DIR keeps its meaning, and
absolute means a drive letter or a UNC prefix; a bare rooted path such as /x
is refused because it carries no disk designator. Write the value natively as
`C:\eUsers\edev\eexports` or `C:/Users/dev/exports`, which works in every
Windows shell. An MSYS\-style `/c/Users/dev/exports` carries no disk
designator and is refused whenever it reaches the process unconverted. Git
Bash and MSYS2 usually convert such a value to native form before a native
binary sees it, but it arrives unconverted when MSYS2_ENV_CONV_EXCL excludes
it, when a process outside that shell exports it, or when a scheduler or
service passes it through.
