.TP
\fB~/.local/share/skarn/compliance\-export/\fR
Compliance API transcript export written by
integrations/anthropic\-compliance/, with one JSON document per session at
<root>/<organization_uuid>/<session_id>.json. $SKARN_COMPLIANCE_EXPORT_DIR
replaces the default root; it must be absolute and non\-empty. When unset, use
$XDG_DATA_HOME/skarn/compliance\-export if XDG_DATA_HOME is absolute, else
this path. The walk enters only directories at the root and descends exactly
one level, so .watermark.json and export\-log.jsonl at the root are never
read. Inside each organization directory, only regular *.json files whose
non\-empty stem uses [A\-Za\-z0\-9_] are read; session.id must match the stem.
A rejected filename, a symlinked session file or a session file with more than
one hard link raises the unreadable\-store count once per store. On Windows,
the default is %LOCALAPPDATA%\eskarn\ecompliance\-export, and it never passes
through ~. XDG_DATA_HOME is not consulted there. LOCALAPPDATA must be an
absolute path with a drive letter or a UNC prefix; otherwise, the source is
dropped and counted: check and assess report a partial scan, doctor reports a
failed source, and the recall commands list nothing for it. A sessions_path
written as `~/...` under tools.claude in ~/.skarn.json resolves through HOME
before USERPROFILE, so a Git Bash or MSYS2 shell that sets HOME moves it.
Setting SKARN_COMPLIANCE_EXPORT_DIR does nothing while that entry stays in
place because the configured path is used before the variable is read. Write
the entry as an absolute path, or remove it so the default and the variable
apply again.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show which license this machine is using, where it came from, and when it
expires.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn license
.EE
.RE
.PP
Validate a license file and install it; the previous license, if any, is
backed up alongside it.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn license ~/Downloads/team.skarnlicense
.EE
.RE
.PP
Fetch a fresh license for this subscription, verify it against the keys built
into this binary, and install it; nothing is written unless it verifies.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn license renew
.EE
.RE
.PP
Scan every AI coding session on this machine and print a friendly risk summary
in one command; no flags, no license.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn assess
.EE
.RE
.PP
Drive the MCP handshake by hand: the server answers one JSON\-RPC line on
stdout and exits 0 when stdin closes.
.RS 4
.EX
printf '%s\en' '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2025\-11\-25","capabilities":{},"clientInfo":{"name":"probe","version":"0"}}}' | skarn mcp
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run the MCP server with the two content tools enabled; put the argument in the
client's own mcpServers args, never in a shipped plugin.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn mcp \-\-enable\-recall
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print what the MCP server exposes and how to register it in a client; it
returns before the protocol loop starts.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn mcp \-\-help
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a self\-contained, redacted security report to share with a colleague;
\-o report.md writes a plain\-text version.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn assess \-o report.html
.EE
.RE
.PP
Statically vet this machine's AI assistant configuration \- hooks, MCP
servers, permission grants, plugins and skills \- and list the risky patterns
found. Read\-only, offline, no license.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn vet
.EE
.RE
.PP
Emit the config findings as SARIF for a code\-scanning pipeline and exit 1 on
anything high or above; an unreadable config file exits 6 rather than
reporting a partial view as clean.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn vet \-\-format sarif \-\-fail\-on\-severity high
.EE
.RE
.PP
Detect installed AI coding assistants and wire the guard hook into each one in
audit mode, with a wizard on a terminal; ends with the guard self\-test and a
list of every file touched.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn setup
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the exact Cursor hooks.json content that setup would write, without
writing anything.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn setup \-\-print \-\-agent cursor
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check that skarn is really protecting this machine: license, every wired
assistant hook, the guard log, and the guard self\-test. Each warning or
failure names the command that fixes it.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn doctor
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same checks as one JSON object for fleet scripts; the check ids are stable
and a rendered report always exits 0 (an unknown option is refused with exit 1
and an unresolvable home directory with exit 6, both before any check runs).
.RS 4
.EX
skarn doctor \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Flip every assistant that already carries skarn entries from audit to enforce,
leaving everything else alone.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn setup \-\-update \-\-mode enforce
.EE
.RE
.PP
Summarize the last 30 days of flagged calls per assistant: verdicts, top
rules, latency, and the most recent denies. When the window is clean, meaning
the flagged calls span five or more days with no deny among them, report also
prints the exact command that flips the hooks to enforce.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn guard report \-\-window 30d
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same aggregate as one JSON object for fleet scripts: counts are over
flagged actions, since the guard does not log clean calls.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn guard report \-\-format json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Accept a finding the guard flagged (the fingerprint printed under the deny in
guard report) as a false positive, so the identical call stops being blocked.
Writes the personal baseline the guard reads; a real leak is never accepted
this way.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn guard accept 7d343e3a105ae026589b3371d565a4ad2943dd5ba5f81a19c7500b2ab4dbb1f0 \-\-reason 'internal test credential'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Prove the guard wiring: run synthetic benign and fake\-credential events
through each adapter's real code path and report the verdicts (credential
shown redacted).
.RS 4
.EX
skarn guard \-\-self\-test
.EE
.RE
.PP
Scan the default window (the last 30 days) of all sessions for leaked secrets
and attack patterns.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn check
.EE
.RE
.PP
Scan all history, report only high\-severity\-and\-above findings, and emit
JSON for a pipeline.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn check \-\-hours 0 \-\-severity high \-\-format json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Exit non\-zero in CI when a session's risk score exceeds 60.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn check \-\-fail\-on\-risk 60
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a redacted Markdown evidence pack for one product build (Pro); redirect
it to a dated file such as skarn\-evidence\-2026\-07\-24.md to file into a
technical file. Omitting the lineage flags renders a pack that states it
cannot be attached to a specific technical file.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn check \-\-format evidence \-\-product acme\-gateway \-\-product\-version 3.2.1 \-\-build\-id 9f2c1ab
.EE
.RE
.PP
Search past sessions for a regular\-expression pattern.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn search 'AWS_SECRET' \-\-regex
.EE
.RE
.PP
List recent Claude Code sessions.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn recent \-\-cli claude
.EE
.RE
.PP
Render per\-project analytics to a self\-contained HTML file.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn stats \-\-by project \-\-format html \-o stats.html
.EE
.RE
.PP
Browse and scan sessions in a local web UI on 127.0.0.1:7777 (localhost only,
no egress); open the token\-carrying URL printed at startup.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn serve \-\-port 7777
.EE
.RE
.PP
Serve the web UI with a custom rule pack layered on the bundled rules; the
Security view surfaces its findings too.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn serve \-\-rules ./team\-rules.toml
.EE
.RE
.PP
Walk each new finding and label it a true or false positive with a reason; the
decisions are written into the committed baseline and survive re\-scans.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn baseline audit .skarn\-baseline.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Record a single false\-positive fingerprint (from SARIF partialFingerprints or
the ndjson skarn.fingerprint field) into the baseline without the interactive
loop.
.RS 4
.EX
skarn baseline accept .skarn\-baseline.json 7d343e3a105ae026589b3371d565a4ad2943dd5ba5f81a19c7500b2ab4dbb1f0 \-\-reason 'internal test credential'
.EE
.RE
.SH SECURITY
Skarn scans locally and makes no network connection by default. Secrets are
redacted in check's reports, in everything the recall commands print (search,
recent, messages, stats, cmds, tools, mcps, restore, export), in the ndjson
stream, the evidence pack, the artifacts written by \-\-drop, serve and
assess, the baseline files, the mcp tools, the guard, and every serve API
route that returns session content. No flag lifts the mask.
.PP
Two commands transmit anything off the machine, and only when you type them.
skarn check \-\-update\-rules fetches a signed feed bundle from the subscriber
channel, presenting $SKARN_FEED_TOKEN as a bearer credential; \-\-feed\-url
names that channel when $SKARN_FEED_URL does not. The feed is off by default
and is disabled by \-\-offline. skarn license renew posts the installed
license token to the license service to receive a re\-signed one. Both refuse
any endpoint that is not https, or http against loopback, and any endpoint
carrying userinfo. No session content, no finding, and no scanned secret ever
leaves the machine through a connection skarn makes. skarn mcp makes no
network connection; the results it hands to the connected MCP client (redacted
findings by default, redacted session content only with \-\-enable\-recall)
are forwarded by that client to its model provider \- that egress is the
client's, and it happens only when you configure the server.
.PP
skarn vet reads the AI assistant configuration files listed under FILES
read\-only: it opens each one for reading, never writes, rewrites, or removes
one, and makes no network call. It reports what it finds and leaves every
decision about the configuration to you. \-\-offline is accepted for parity
and changes nothing.
.PP
skarn mcp reads the same session stores as assess and the same configuration
files as vet, read\-only and with no network call, and answers only the AI
coding assistant that spawned it over stdin and stdout. Its results are the
redacted scan report, the masked vet report, and session metadata carrying
neither message text nor any label authored inside a transcript; with
\-\-enable\-recall the two content tools add session text and labels with
every detected secret masked, and the search response carries no echo of the
query or of the project filter. Because the assistant receives those results,
they travel wherever that assistant sends its context, so treat them as
leaving the machine even though skarn itself opens no socket.
